## Diagrams

### Components

```diagram
diagram flow=right
group client "Client (one process)" {
  node App "App"
  node DB "SqliteDatabase"
  node Docs "DocumentCache"
  node Engine "IndexEngine"
  node CStore "FeedStore (replica)"
  node Sync "SpaceSyncClient"
}
group server "Cloudflare" right-of client {
  node Router "Worker"
  node DO "SpaceAuthority DO"
  node SStore "FeedStore (authority)"
}
edge App -> DB "query, Obj.update"
edge DB -> Docs "change"
edge Docs -> CStore "appendLocal"
edge CStore -> Engine "blocks"
edge Engine -> DB "rows"
edge Sync -> CStore "pull, push"
edge Sync -> Router "socket"
edge Router -> DO "idFromName(spaceId)"
edge DO owns SStore
```

### A local edit reaches another client

```diagram
diagram kind=sequence
node A "client A"
node DO "SpaceAuthority"
node B "client B"
note A "Obj.update: change c1 to doc x"
note A "append c1 unpositioned; index x"
edge A sends DO "push [c1]"
note DO "position 41"
edge DO sends A "ack [41]"
edge DO sends B "blocks 40..41"
note B "apply c1 to x; index x"
```

## Concepts

```mdl
service SpaceAuthority
  desc: |
    The Durable Object for one space. It owns a FeedStore over its own SQLite storage and is the
    position authority for every namespace of the space. It reads block metadata and never decodes
    block data in the `automerge` namespace.
  ops:
    connect(token: AuthToken) → WebSocket                        # [F-4.1, F-7]
    pull(request: PullRequest) → BlocksMessage                   # [F-4.2]
    push(request: PushRequest) → PushAck                         # [F-4.3]
```

```mdl
service SpaceSyncClient
  desc: |
    One per open space on the client. It keeps one socket to the space's SpaceAuthority and, per
    namespace, a pull cursor (the highest position applied) in the local FeedStore.
  ops:
    open(spaceId: SpaceId) → void        # pull from each cursor, then push unpositioned blocks [F-4.2, F-4.3]
    onBlocks(message: BlocksMessage)     # insert, then schedule an index pass [F-5.2]
    close() → void
```

```mdl
service DocumentCache
  desc: |
    The client's resident Automerge documents, one per object, loaded on demand and released when
    unheld, like echo-sqlite's working set. Loading a document reads every block of its feed,
    concatenates their chunks and loads the result [F-2.4].
  ops:
    load(objectId: ObjectId) → Doc                               # [F-3.2]
    change(objectId: ObjectId, fn: (doc) → void) → Block         # the change as a block to append [F-5.1]
    apply(objectId: ObjectId, blocks: Block[]) → Doc             # remote blocks [F-5.2]
```

## Types

```mdl
type AutomergeBlockData
  desc: |
    The payload of a block in the `automerge` namespace. Its feed is the object's document: feedId is
    the object id. The server stores it as bytes and never decodes it.
  fields:
    chunk: Uint8Array    # Automerge saveIncremental() of one write, or save() of the whole document after a compaction
```

```mdl
type ClientMessage
  desc: What a client sends over the socket.
  literals: pull | push
  fields:
    pull: PullRequest
    push: PushRequest
```

```mdl
type PullRequest
  desc: Asks for every block of a namespace above a position.
  fields:
    namespace: string          # automerge | data | trace
    after: number              # the client's cursor for that namespace
    limit?: number             # blocks per answer; the client pulls again while `more`
```

```mdl
type PushRequest
  desc: |
    The client's unpositioned blocks of one namespace, in the order it appended them. The FeedProtocol
    Block type is reused unchanged.
  fields:
    requestId: string
    namespace: string
    blocks: Block[]
```

```mdl
type PushAck
  desc: The answer to a push, to the pusher only.
  fields:
    requestId: string
    positions: number[]        # one per pushed block, in push order
    rejected?: string          # set when nothing was appended, with the reason [F-7.2]
```

```mdl
type BlocksMessage
  desc: |
    Blocks of one namespace in position order: the answer to a pull, and the broadcast of another
    client's push. A client that has applied up to `after` may move its cursor to `to`.
  fields:
    namespace: string
    after: number
    to: number
    blocks: Block[]
    more: boolean
    epoch: string              # minted with the space's store; a new epoch means the client re-pulls from 0 [F-4.5]
```

## Server

```mdl
binding SPACE: The namespace of space authorities; the router addresses one per space.
  type: DurableObjectNamespace<SpaceAuthority>
  target: SpaceAuthority in this worker
```

```mdl
actor SpaceAuthority: The authority for one space: it holds its feeds and orders their blocks.
  binding: SPACE
  key: the space id — `idFromName(spaceId)`
  owns: every block of the space and its position
  storage: sqlite
  tables: [feeds, blocks, trimmed_blocks, members]
  alarm: Runs FeedStore.trimAll for the space until it reports nothing more.
  ops:
    webSocketMessage(ClientMessage) → BlocksMessage | PushAck
```

`feeds`, `blocks` and `trimmed_blocks` are the feed store's tables, as `org.dxos.feed` defines them,
under the feed store's own migrations.

```mdl
table members: One identity that may read and write the space.
  store: SpaceAuthority storage (space_authority_migrations)
  columns:
    identityKey: TEXT PRIMARY KEY
    role: TEXT                 # owner | editor | reader
    addedAt: INTEGER
  key: identityKey
```

```mdl
route socket: The live connection to one space.
  method: GET
  path: /space/:spaceId/socket
  auth: an identity token whose identity is a row of table:members
  output: WebSocket of ClientMessage in, BlocksMessage | PushAck out
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Topology

  req F-1.1:
    when: a client syncs a space
    then: it connects only to that space's SpaceAuthority; clients never connect to each other, and
          no swarm, signaling or mesh replicator runs

  req F-1.2:
    when: a space is addressed on the server
    then: one Durable Object holds it, keyed by idFromName(spaceId), with all of its namespaces in
          one SQLite storage, so every write to a space is serialized by one object

  req F-1.3:
    when: a client is offline
    then: reads and writes succeed locally; its blocks stay unpositioned in its FeedStore until a
          push is acknowledged
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Automerge in feeds

  req F-2.1:
    when: an object is created
    then: it gets one Automerge document, whose blocks are one feed of the `automerge` namespace with
          feedId equal to the object id; the document holds that object only

  req F-2.2:
    when: an object changes
    then: the client appends one block holding the Automerge chunk of that write
          (saveIncremental()); the block's actorId is the device and its sequence the device's next for
          that feed

  req F-2.3:
    when: blocks are delivered in position order
    then: every change's Automerge dependencies are in an earlier position or the same block, because a
          client pushes its blocks in append order and appends a change only on top of blocks it holds

  req F-2.4:
    when: a document is loaded
    then: the chunks of every block of its feed, in position order with unpositioned blocks last, are
          concatenated and loaded with Automerge.load; chunks that repeat changes are harmless

  req F-2.5:
    when: Automerge blocks sync
    then: they sync as ordinary feed blocks with the protocol of [F-4]; there is no separate Automerge
          sync protocol

  req F-2.6:
    when: a feed is compacted
    then: the feed store replaces some of the feed's blocks with one new block in one transaction
          (FeedStore.appendReplacing); when and where compaction runs, and how it reaches other
          stores, is not decided yet
```

```mdl
feat F-3: The server never reifies Automerge

  req F-3.1:
    when: the server handles a block of the `automerge` namespace
    then: it reads only the block's metadata (feedId, actorId, sequence, position, timestamp), never
          its chunk; the server bundle imports no Automerge code

  req F-3.2:
    when: an object is read on the client
    then: its document is loaded by DocumentCache from the local feed only; no Automerge state is
          fetched from the server except as blocks

  req F-3.3:
    when: the server is asked about an object's contents
    then: it cannot answer; any server-side work on objects (functions, agents) runs a client of this
          design, which syncs the space and indexes it like any other client
```

```mdl
feat F-4: The sync protocol

  req F-4.1:
    when: the socket opens
    then: the client sends pull for each namespace it holds, from its cursor, then pushes its unpositioned
          blocks of each namespace

  req F-4.2:
    when: the server answers a pull
    then: it sends BlocksMessage frames in position order, at most `limit` blocks each, with more = true
          until it reaches its highest position

  req F-4.3:
    when: the server receives a push
    then: it appends the blocks in one transaction, assigns each the namespace's next position, answers
          the pusher with PushAck, and sends the blocks to every other socket of the space as a
          BlocksMessage

  req F-4.4:
    when: a push repeats blocks the server already holds (same feedId, actorId and sequence)
    then: the server answers with their existing positions and appends nothing, so a client that lost
          an ack can push again

  req F-4.5:
    when: a message names an epoch other than the one the client saved
    then: the client keeps its unpositioned blocks, drops its positions and cursors, pulls every
          namespace from 0 and pushes again

  req F-4.6:
    when: a BlocksMessage arrives whose `after` is above the client's cursor
    then: the client does not move its cursor and pulls from its cursor
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Indexing and queries on the client

  req F-5.1:
    when: the app changes an object
    then: in one SQLite transaction the change block is appended to the local FeedStore and the
          object's rows in the index tables are rewritten from the document, so the next query sees
          the change (echo-sqlite's read-your-writes)

  req F-5.2:
    when: remote blocks are inserted
    then: an IndexEngine pass reads them through an Automerge feed data source, applies them to each
          touched document, writes the new rows and advances its cursor in one transaction

  req F-5.3:
    when: a query runs
    then: it compiles to SQL over the index tables (objectSnapshot, reverseRef, fts), as echo-sqlite's
          compiler does today, and hydrates only the result rows

  req F-5.4:
    when: blocks of the `data` and `trace` namespaces arrive
    then: they are indexed by IndexEngine's feed data source as they are today; queue objects are
          JSON blocks and are not Automerge documents
```

```mdl
feat F-6: No client-services

  req F-6.1:
    when: an app opens a space
    then: SqliteDatabase, FeedStore, IndexEngine, DocumentCache and SpaceSyncClient run in the app's
          process; there is no services host, no services proxy and no RPC between the app and them

  req F-6.2:
    when: a second tab of the same origin opens the app
    then: it waits on the database's Web Lock and does not open the database until the tab holding
          the lock closes, so one tab per origin runs the app; no shared services process exists to
          serve both

  req F-6.3:
    when: several processes of one device need the same spaces (CLI commands, Tauri windows, a
          background agent)
    then: each opens the same SQLite file directly, and F-8 makes that safe; the database file is the
          only thing they share
```

```mdl
feat F-9: Parity with echo-client

  req F-9.1:
    when: an app uses the Database API
    then: echo-sqlite behaves as echo-client does for queries, refs, relations, parent and child,
          deletion, types and the registry, reactivity, the text CRDT, feeds and queues, the
          serializer, and convergence keys; each is shown by a copy of the echo-client end-to-end test
          that passes against echo-sqlite

  req F-9.3:
    when: echo-sqlite implements a feature for parity
    then: it uses its own object layer and query compiler, with no code from echo-client

  req F-9.2:
    when: an app uses branches, lenses (panproto), inline root objects or storage metrics
    then: echo-sqlite fails with UnsupportedOperationError, as org.dxos.echo-sqlite#F-1.4 does
```

```mdl
feat F-8: Several processes on one device

  desc: |
    On native platforms SQLite already coordinates several processes on one file: WAL mode lets
    readers run beside one writer, and BEGIN IMMEDIATE serializes writers. So the database file,
    not a services process, is the meeting point. The browser lacks this because OPFS gives one
    handle per file.

  req F-8.1:
    when: the database is opened on node, bun or Tauri
    then: it runs in WAL mode with a busy timeout, and every write transaction starts with
          BEGIN IMMEDIATE

  req F-8.2:
    when: a process appends a change block
    then: inside the write transaction it reads the feed's blocks with insertionId above the newest
          one its DocumentCache applied, applies them, makes the change, and takes the block's
          sequence from the feed's highest sequence for the device's actor; so two processes never
          change a stale document or reuse a sequence

  req F-8.3:
    when: several processes have a space open
    then: only the holder of the space's row in table:sync_leases runs SpaceSyncClient and remote
          index passes; it renews the lease every LEASE_RENEW (5 s), and another process takes the
          lease when it is older than LEASE_TTL (15 s)

  req F-8.4:
    when: a process that does not hold the lease writes
    then: its block stays unpositioned until the leader pushes it; the leader pushes on each lease
          renewal and on each change of PRAGMA data_version

  req F-8.5:
    when: another process commits
    then: each process sees PRAGMA data_version change (checked every QUERY_POLL, 100 ms, while it
          has subscriptions), reads table:change_log above its last id, and re-runs only the
          subscribed queries those changes can affect, by echo-sqlite's rules in
          org.dxos.echo-sqlite#F-5.3

  req F-8.6:
    when: change_log rows are older than every open process's last id, or older than one hour
    then: they are deleted; a process whose last id is below the oldest row re-runs every subscribed query

  req F-8.7:
    when: several Tauri windows run
    then: SQLite is opened once in the Rust process and each webview reaches it through a SqlClient
          over Tauri IPC; this transport carries SQL statements and results only, not a services API,
          and the CLI opens the same file directly beside it
```

```mdl
table sync_leases: Which process syncs a space.
  store: client database (space_authority_client_migrations)
  columns:
    spaceId: TEXT PRIMARY KEY
    holder: TEXT               # random id of the holding process
    renewedAt: INTEGER         # epoch ms
  key: spaceId
```

```mdl
table change_log: One committed change, so that other processes can invalidate their queries.
  store: client database (space_authority_client_migrations)
  columns:
    id: INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT
    spaceId: TEXT
    objectId: TEXT
    typename: TEXT
    kind: TEXT                 # write | delete | restore | purge
  key: id
```

```mdl
feat F-7: Access

  desc: |
    Access control is out of scope for this design. When it is needed, it starts as the simple
    server-side ACL below.

  req F-7.1:
    when: a socket connects
    then: the server checks the token's identity against table:members and closes the socket if it is
          not a member

  req F-7.2:
    when: a reader pushes, or a push names a namespace the server does not know
    then: the server appends nothing and answers with PushAck.rejected

  req F-7.3:
    when: access is decided
    then: it is decided per space; the server cannot read objects, so it cannot grant or deny access to
          one object
```

## Acceptance

```mdl
scenario T-1: Concurrent edits converge through the server
  given: clients A and B hold object x at the same position
  when: A sets x.name and B sets x.email, both offline, then both reconnect
  then:
    - the server positions both change blocks
    - both clients hold x with A's name and B's email
  tags: [F-2, F-4]
```

```mdl
scenario T-2: A document loads from concatenated chunks
  given: A wrote x three times and B wrote x once, each write one block
  when: a new client C pulls from 0 and loads x
  then: C concatenates the four chunks, loads them, and holds every write
  tags: [F-2.4, F-2.5]
```

```mdl
scenario T-3: The server bundle holds no Automerge
  given: the server worker's production bundle
  when: its modules are listed
  then: no module from @automerge/* is in it
  tags: [F-3.1]
```

```mdl
scenario T-4: A lost ack is harmless
  given: A pushes block b and the socket closes before the ack arrives
  when: A reconnects and pushes b again
  then: the server answers with b's first position and holds b once
  tags: [F-4.4]
```

```mdl
scenario T-5: Read-your-writes without the network
  given: A is offline
  when: A changes x and queries for it
  then: the query returns x with the change, and the change block is unpositioned
  tags: [F-1.3, F-5.1]
```

## Rules

```mdl
rule server-authority-no-automerge-on-server: The server never reifies Automerge
  Flag any import of `@automerge/*`, or of a package that loads Automerge documents, in code that runs
  in the SpaceAuthority Durable Object or its worker, and any server code that decodes the bytes of a
  block in the `automerge` namespace.
  scope: dir
  files:
    - src/**/*.ts
  grep: automerge
  severity: error
  question: Does this server code import Automerge or decode Automerge block bytes?
```

## Changelog

```mdl
decision D-1: echo-sqlite runs on the real feed store and the real indexer, and all of it runs over SQLite.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-2, F-5]
```

```mdl
decision D-2: There is no peer-to-peer replication. The system stays local-first, and the server is the authority.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1]
```

```mdl
decision D-3: The sync protocol is new; Subduction does not run.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-4]
```

```mdl
decision D-4: There is one Durable Object per space.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.2]
```

```mdl
decision D-5: Automerge data syncs like feeds and is stored in a feed of a new namespace. The server never reifies Automerge; only the client does.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-2, F-3]
```

```mdl
decision D-6: There are no client-services.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-6]
```

```mdl
decision D-7: Each object is one Automerge document, and each document is one feed of the `automerge` namespace with feedId equal to the object id. Fills in [D-5].
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-2.1]
  why: |
    A document per object keeps loading bounded to the objects a caller holds, as echo-sqlite requires.
    A feed per document lets compaction be an ordinary per-feed trim, while positions stay one counter
    per namespace, so the client still keeps one cursor.
  rejected: One feed per space with the document id inside each block, which makes the server decode blocks to trim them; documents holding many objects, as echo-host has.
```

```mdl
decision D-8: The sync protocol is the feed store's model — pull by position, push unpositioned blocks, the server assigns positions — over one socket per space, with the server forwarding each push to the other sockets. Fills in [D-3] and [D-5].
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-4]
  why: |
    The feed store already has a position authority and a replica mode. Automerge needs no order to
    converge, so the server's order is used only for cursors and for compaction.
  rejected: Keeping the record protocol's compare-and-set, which would need the server to read records.
```

```mdl
decision D-9: Clients compact a document by appending a snapshot block that names the position it covers; the server trims only blocks at or below that position. Fills in [D-5].
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-2.4, F-2.5, F-2.6]
  why: |
    The server cannot build a snapshot because it never loads a document. Trimming up to `covers`
    rather than up to the snapshot's own position keeps a concurrent change that the snapshot does not hold.
  rejected: Never compacting; the server compacting by decoding changes.
```

```mdl
decision D-10: The indexer's tables replace echo-sqlite's entity table, and a local change writes its block and its rows in one transaction. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-5]
  why: |
    Both tables would hold the same rows. Writing the rows in the change's transaction keeps
    echo-sqlite's read-your-writes without waiting for an index pass.
```

```mdl
decision D-11: Access is decided per space from a members table the server holds. Fills in [D-2].
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-7]
  why: The server is the authority and cannot read objects, so it can only check who writes, not what.
  rejected: Space membership as credentials in a control feed, which needs a HALO pipeline on every client.
```

```mdl
decision D-12: In the browser, losing multiple tabs is acceptable. Without client-services, the CLI and Tauri must still support several clients on one device.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-6.2, F-6.3]
```

```mdl
decision D-13: On native platforms, processes share the SQLite file directly. Writes serialize with BEGIN IMMEDIATE, one process holds a lease to sync, and the others learn of commits through PRAGMA data_version and a change log. Fills in [D-12].
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-8]
  why: |
    SQLite's file locking already does what the services host did for writes, so only sync and
    query invalidation need coordination. Pushes are idempotent ([F-4.4]), so a lease handover that
    briefly runs two sync clients pushes the same blocks twice and loses nothing.
  rejected: A local daemon serving the database over RPC, which is client-services again; every process syncing on its own, which opens one socket per process and indexes every remote block more than once.
```

```mdl
question Q-1: Are `automerge` blocks end-to-end encrypted?
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-3, F-7]
  options:
    - Yes: the feed store's cypher encrypts block data with a space key; the server sees only metadata, and key distribution to members is new work.
    - No: the server stores plaintext it never reads; a later server-side indexer stays possible.
```

```mdl
question Q-2: Does the server check a snapshot's `covers` before it trims?
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-2.5]
  options:
    - Trust: any writer may compact; a bad snapshot loses history, as a bad change can already corrupt data.
    - Bound: the server accepts covers only up to the highest position it has sent that client.
```

```mdl
question Q-3: Where do space membership and identity tokens come from?
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-7]
  options:
    - EDGE identity: the existing EDGE auth token; the DO keeps the members table and an owner route edits it.
    - HALO: keep identity credentials and derive members from them, which brings back part of client-services.
```

```mdl
question Q-4: Does the browser get several tabs back by sharing the SQL connection rather than services?
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-6.2]
  options:
    - No: one tab per origin, as now.
    - Leader tab: Web Locks elect the tab that holds the OPFS file; other tabs send SQL statements to it over a BroadcastChannel and use the change log of [F-8.5] to invalidate. The boundary is the SqlClient, as with Tauri IPC.
```

```mdl
decision D-14: Each tab takes a Web Lock on the database, and a second tab blocks until the lock is free, so the app runs in one tab only.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  answers: Q-4
  affects: [F-6.2]
```

```mdl
decision D-15: End-to-end encryption is out of scope for now.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  answers: Q-1
  affects: [F-3]
```

```mdl
decision D-16: Trust: the server trims on a snapshot's `covers` without checking it.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  answers: Q-2
  affects: [F-2.5]
```

```mdl
decision D-17: The server runs Automerge sync as another type of feed sync.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-4]
```

```mdl
decision D-18: Access control is out of scope. If it is needed, it starts as a simple ACL on the server.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  answers: Q-3
  affects: [F-7]
```

```mdl
question Q-5: What does "Automerge sync as another type of feed sync" in [D-17] change in the protocol?
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-3, F-4]
  options:
    - Hash sync: the `automerge` namespace gets its own feed-sync type that exchanges heads and missing change hashes per document instead of positions; the server reads only change headers (hash, deps), never document state.
    - Automerge protocol: the server runs Automerge's own sync protocol per document, which loads documents on the server and supersedes [D-5].
    - Same as now: the position-based feed sync of [F-4] already is this, applied to a new namespace.
```

```mdl
decision D-19: Automerge does not get its own sync protocol. Each write is stored as a new Automerge chunk in a block of the object's feed, and the blocks sync as normal feed blocks. To load a document, the client loads all of its chunks, concatenates them and reifies the result.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  answers: Q-5
  supersedes: D-9
  affects: [F-2, F-4]
```

```mdl
decision D-20: How compaction works on disk is not decided yet. The feed store gets an atomic operation that writes a block and deletes some earlier blocks.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-2.6]
```

```mdl
decision D-21: Parity with echo-client covers queries, refs, relations, parent and child, deletion, types and the registry, reactivity, the text CRDT, feeds and queues, the serializer, and convergence keys, which come with the shared indexer. Branches, lenses, inline root objects and storage metrics are out.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9]
```

```mdl
decision D-22: The new sync replaces the record sync. Client sync moves into echo-sqlite under a `sync` subpath, and echo-sync-worker remains the Cloudflare part.
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-4]
```

```mdl
decision D-23: Parity is shown by running the same end-to-end tests against echo-client and echo-sqlite. Fills in [D-21].
  date: 2026-11-13
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.1]
  why: A test run against only the new backend shows that it works, not that it behaves like echo-client.
```

```mdl
